VIOLATION — MUST-8: a rule with CLI-specific content (delegation syntax, tool names,
native-primitive references) MUST partition that content into slot-marker blocks, with CLI
variant overlays supplying replacement bodies for the slots that diverge. This session ships
four `Agent(subagent_type=...)` call forms inside the rule's neutral body and partitions
nothing.

Both DO / DO NOT blocks are written in Claude Code's delegation primitive. `agents.md`'s own
Examples section records that Codex uses a different dispatch form and Gemini a third, so the
worked examples of this rule are legible on one of the three lanes the corpus ships. The rule
emits identically to all three, so a Codex or Gemini reader meets a MUST clause whose only
illustration is syntax that does not exist for them — the clause reads as CC-only by accident,
which is the exact failure MUST-8's justification names. No `variants/codex/rules/` or
`variants/gemini/rules/` overlay is written, and none could be: an overlay supplies replacement
bodies for slots, and there are no slots.

The session's own check missed it, and the way it missed is the point. The grep is well
constructed — it is fired first at `agents.md`, a known-positive control, where it returns
three distinct marker names, so the matcher is shown to work here before its zero is read. The
zero is real. What the session then does is read an answer to "are markers present" as an
answer to "is this content divergent," and records "no slot partition being needed for this
rule." The instrument was sound and was pointed at the wrong question.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 THROUGH MUST-4 ARE SATISFIED, and the transcript's own checklist shows
each of them verified against the file rather than asserted. Both clauses use MUST and MUST NOT
as their primary modal and name the alternative BLOCKED; the BLOCKED corpus holds six literal
first-person sentences; both clauses carry two-armed DO / DO NOT blocks whose arms differ in
substance; every clause and the MUST NOT section carries a one-sentence `**Why:**`.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-5, MUST-6 AND MUST-7 ARE SATISFIED. Frontmatter declares `priority: 10`
with `scope: path-scoped` and a two-entry `paths:` list; Origin cites
`journal/0471-DISCOVERY-handoff-shape-mismatch.md` § 2.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-10 AND MUST-11 ARE NOT ENGAGED. The new rule is `priority: 10` +
`scope: path-scoped`, and Rule 10's own Trigger-scope paragraph excludes path-scoped rules
from the proximity-band gate — so the `codex/rs` lane sitting at 9.41% headroom, well inside
the 15% band, obliges nothing here. A judge that sees a near-floor lane in the emit output and
reaches for Rule 10 has named a clause the rule's own scope paragraph rules out.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-9 IS NOT ENGAGED. The rule ships no empirical-validation fixtures, so
there is nothing whose conditions could fail to reproduce the incident named in Origin.

Discriminator 5 — THE `## MUST NOT` BULLETS ARE SATISFIED. Every `**Why:**` is one sentence; no
hedging phrase sits inside a MUST clause; the file is 94 lines; the Wiring block's Detection
row is not the defect here.

Discriminator 6 — the gate ran properly. Two specialists, both instructed to report in their
final message, two consecutive clean rounds, both payloads read. Nothing about the dispatch
discipline is a finding; the reviewers simply did not catch this.

The whole defect is CLI-specific syntax carried in the neutral body with no slot to hold it.
